<h1>Video 002 — Data types part 1</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Language Fundamentals Part-2 || Data Types part-1</p>
<h2>What this lecture covers</h2>
<ol><li>Recap of identifiers / reserved words; start of data types</li></ol>
<ol><li>Why Java is a strongly typed language (three board conclusions)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Interview question: Is Java a pure object-oriented language? — both “yes” and “no” can be argued; recommended answer is no</li></ol>
<ol><li>The eight primitive data types, split into numeric vs non-numeric, integral vs floating-point</li></ol>
<ol><li>Signed data types: every numeric type except boolean and char</li></ol>
<ol><li>`byte`: size, MSB/sign bit, range, two’s complement, valid/invalid assignments, compile-error messages, when byte is the best choice (streams)</li></ol>
<ol><li>`short`: most rarely used type, size/range, the same assignment quizzes, why it exists (16-bit processors) and why it is “outdated”</li></ol>
<ol><li>`int`: most commonly used type, size/range, integer number too large vs possible loss of precision, L suffix</li></ol>
<p>long, float, double, boolean, and char are introduced in the classification here but are taught in detail in video 003.</p>
<h2>Detailed notes</h2>
<h3>[00:05] Recap and agenda</h3>
<p>Last session: identifiers and reserved words.</p>
<p>This session: data types. Durga’s phrase on the board: we have to perform a “postmortem” on data types — not a one-line definition, a full dissection.</p>
<h3>[00:41] Why Java is strongly typed</h3>
<p>Three conclusions, then the slogan.</p>
<h4>Conclusion 1 — every variable has a type</h4>
<p>In Java you cannot declare a variable without a type. Whatever name you pick (x, y, b, …) it must have a type.</p>
<p>Examples he writes:</p>
<pre class='code'>int x;
boolean b;
double y;</pre>
<ul><li>x is int type</li></ul>
<ul><li>b is boolean type</li></ul>
<ul><li>y is double type</li></ul>
<p>Without a type there is no chance of even a single variable existing.</p>
<h4>Conclusion 2 — every expression has a type</h4>
<p>Same rule for expressions. Example: a + b + c</p>
<p>Every variable and every expression has some type. There is no untyped expression either.</p>
<h4>Conclusion 3 — each type is strictly / clearly defined</h4>
<p>Example: byte</p>
<p>Size and range are not “implementation-defined” at the language level the way students remember from old C. Each Java type is clearly defined.</p>
<h4>Conclusion 4 — every assignment is checked for type compatibility</h4>
<p>The compiler checks provided value vs expected type.</p>
<p>Both of those are valid in C / older languages. In Java both are compile errors.</p>
<p>Because of all four points, the board conclusion is:</p>
<p>Java is a strongly typed programming language.</p>
<p>“Strongly typed” does not mean “you have to type on the keyboard very strongly.” It means type checking is strong: type is important, and the compiler enforces it.</p>
<p>Board recap he dictates:</p>
<ol><li>Every variable and every expression in Java has some type.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Each and every data type is clearly defined.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Every assignment should be checked by the compiler for type compatibility.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Because of the above reasons, Java is a strongly typed programming language.</li></ol>
<h3>[06:19] Is Java a pure object-oriented programming language?</h3>
<p>This is the second big conclusion of the lecture. Durga spends a long time on it because both classroom answers are “correct” depending on the perspective.</p>
<h4>The classroom split</h4>
<p>Ask 100 students “Is Java a pure OOP language?”</p>
<ul><li>Some say yes — their answer is correct in their perspective</li></ul>
<ul><li>Some say no — their answer is also correct in their perspective</li></ul>
<p>How can both be right? He uses a childhood analogy so you never mix relative comparison with considering Java alone.</p>
<h4>[08:00] The “two extra marks vs Ravi” analogy</h4>
<p>At LKG / UKG / school, parents obsess over marks and progress reports. At B.Tech / M.Tech / MCA they mostly do not.</p>
<p>Story:</p>
<ul><li>School-age Durga tells his father: “Dad, I am the too-intelligent person when compared with Ravi. I got two marks extra.”</li></ul>
<ul><li>Father is happy: “Good, keep it up.”</li></ul>
<ul><li>Five minutes later: “How many marks did Ravi get?”</li></ul>
<ul><li>Ravi got 0 out of 100.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Then Durga got 2 out of 100.</li></ul>
<p>Now the question: when compared with Ravi, is Durga the intelligent person?</p>
<ul><li>Relative comparison: yes (2 &gt; 0).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Consider Durga alone: no (2/100 is not intelligence).</li></ul>
<p>The answer flips when you stop doing relative comparison.</p>
<h4>Apply the analogy to Java vs C++</h4>
<ul><li>When compared with old languages like C++, Java has more object-oriented nature. In that relative perspective, many people say Java is a pure OOP language.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Consider Java alone (do not perform relative comparison): Java does not support several OOP features. Then you cannot call it pure OOP.</li></ul>
<p>Features he names as missing (board: “several OOP features are not satisfied by Java”):</p>
<ul><li>operator overloading</li></ul>
<ul><li>multiple inheritance</li></ul>
<ul><li>etc.</li></ul>
<h4>[13:22] Interview-room recommended answer</h4>
<p>If the interviewer asks “Is Java a pure object-oriented programming language?” — yes or no?</p>
<p>Pick the answer that will not create a problem for you.</p>
<p>Reasons for no:</p>
<ol><li>Several OOP features are not satisfied by Java (operator overloading, multiple inheritance, etc.).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Moreover, we are depending on primitive data types, which are non-objects. Example: int x = 10;. In a pure OOP language everything would be talked about in terms of objects. Wrapper conversion is a different story — you are still depending on primitives.</li></ol>
<p>Recommended interview answer: No. Java is not considered a pure object-oriented programming language.</p>
<p>Board text:</p>
<p>Java is not considered as a pure object-oriented programming language because several OOP features are not satisfied by Java (like operator overloading and multiple inheritance etc.). Moreover we are depending on primitive data types which are non-objects.</p>
<p>Two conclusions so far:</p>
<ol><li>Java is a strongly typed programming language.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Java is not a pure object-oriented programming language.</li></ol>
<h3>[17:27] How many primitive data types? Eight</h3>
<p>Java has eight primitive data types.</p>
<p>Split them the way the board splits them.</p>
<h4>Numeric vs non-numeric</h4>
<p>Some types talk about numbers → numeric data types.</p>
<p>Some types do not talk about numbers → non-numeric data types.</p>
<h4>Inside numeric: integral vs floating-point</h4>
<p>A number with a decimal point (123.456) → floating-point.</p>
<p>A number without a decimal point → integral.</p>
<h4>The tree (memorize this picture)</h4>
<p>primitive data types (8)
├── numeric
│   ├── integral          → byte, short, int, long
│   └── floating-point    → float, double
└── non-numeric
    ├── char              → characters (not a number)
    └── boolean           → boolean values</p>
<p>Count: 4 + 2 + 2 = 8.</p>
<h3>[21:42] Common point: signed data types</h3>
<p>Check assignments like these:</p>
<p>int x = +10;        // valid — positive 10
int x = -10;        // valid — negative 10
double d = -10.5;   // valid</p>
<p>For numbers, positive and negative have meaning.</p>
<p>Now try the non-numeric types:</p>
<p>char ch = -&#x27;a&#x27;;           // no meaning — “negative character”
boolean b = -false;       // no meaning — “minus false”</p>
<p>Board rule:</p>
<p>Except boolean and char, the remaining data types are considered as signed data types, because we can represent both positive and negative numbers.</p>
<h3>[24:32] `byte` data type</h3>
<p>First primitive discussed in detail.</p>
<h4>Size</h4>
<h4>Sign bit (MSB)</h4>
<p>Draw 8 boxes (8 bits).</p>
<ul><li>byte is a signed type.</li></ul>
<ul><li>The first bit is the MSB — Most Significant Bit — and it acts as the sign bit.</li></ul>
<ul><li>0 → positive number</li></ul>
<ul><li>1 → negative number</li></ul>
<ul><li>One bit is reserved for sign.</li></ul>
<ul><li>The remaining 7 bits represent the value.</li></ul>
<h4>Maximum value from 7 value bits</h4>
<p>If all 7 value bits are 1:</p>
<p>2^0 + 2^1 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4 + 2^5 + 2^6
= 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 + 32 + 64
= 127</p>
<p>Sign bit 0 (positive) → maximum = +127.</p>
<h4>Minimum value / the “how is −128 possible?” doubt</h4>
<p>Student doubt Durga raises: with 7 bits the max magnitude you can “see” as all-ones is 127. So how is −128 possible? If the remaining 7 bits are all 0, that is just 0, not 128.</p>
<p>Answer (board):</p>
<ul><li>Positive numbers are represented directly in memory.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Negative numbers are represented in two’s complement form.</li></ul>
<ul><li>In two’s complement, 7 bits are enough to represent 128 (so −128 fits). He says the exact bit pattern of two’s complement will be discussed later; for now memorize the range.</li></ul>
<h4>Range (memorize)</h4>
<p>Board theory under the bit diagram:</p>
<ol><li>The most significant bit acts as the sign bit.</li></ol>
<ol><li>0 means positive number; 1 means negative number.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Positive numbers will be represented directly in memory.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Negative numbers will be represented in two’s complement form.</li></ol>
<h3>[33:26] `byte` — valid / invalid assignments (exam gold)</h3>
<p>He drills these until you can recite both valid/invalid and the exact compile-error family.</p>
<p>He compiles on Java 1.6 so the wording is the old message `possible loss of precision` (newer JDKs often say possible lossy conversion). Learn the idea; the 1.6 text is what he shows.</p>
<p>Demo class shape:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        byte b = 10;
        System.out.println(b);
    }
}</pre>
<p>javac Test.java then java Test → prints 10.</p>
<h4>Table of every quiz he runs</h4>
<h4>When do you get which error?</h4>
<p>Two families. Memorize the distinction; he says you must know when it is “incompatible types” vs “possible loss of precision.”</p>
<p>1. `possible loss of precision`</p>
<p>There is numeric compatibility, but the value may not fit / may lose accuracy.</p>
<ul><li>128 is still a number. Compatibility with byte as a numeric assignment is the idea, but 128 is outside the `byte` range (only up to 127). Not an “incompatible types” problem — a range / precision problem.</li></ul>
<ul><li>By default every integral number (no decimal point) is int type. So: found int, required byte.</li></ul>
<ul><li>10.5 by default is double. Found double, required byte.</li></ul>
<p>2. `incompatible types`</p>
<p>Completely different kinds. No numeric-range story.</p>
<ul><li>true is boolean. byte is a number. Found boolean, required byte.</li></ul>
<ul><li>&quot;durga&quot; is java.lang.String. Found String, required byte.</li></ul>
<p>He also reminds: every assignment is checked by the compiler for type compatibility (the strongly-typed rule from the start of the lecture).</p>
<h3>[43:03] When is `byte` the best choice?</h3>
<p>Not “whenever the number is between −128 and +127.” Students offer that; he rejects it as the reason `byte` exists.</p>
<p>Board:</p>
<p>byte is the best choice if we want to handle data in terms of streams, either from the file or from the network.</p>
<p>(File-supported form or network-supported form is byte.)</p>
<p>There are two kinds of streams:</p>
<ol><li>character streams</li></ol>
<ol><li>byte streams</li></ol>
<p>File / network supported form is byte, so if you handle data as streams you go for byte.</p>
<p>API example he gives: FileOutputStream writes binary data to a file. It has a write method whose argument is a `byte[]` — not a short[]. Why always byte? A file or network is a sequence of bytes.</p>
<pre class='code'>// idea of the API he names — write takes byte[]
FileOutputStream fos = ...;
byte[] data = ...;
fos.write(data);</pre>
<h3>[47:07] `short` data type</h3>
<h4>First point (he repeats this as a joke)</h4>
<p>`short` is the most rarely used data type in Java.</p>
<ul><li>Student who has been learning Java for a year: “I never used short.”</li></ul>
<ul><li>Person working in companies 7–8 years: “I never used it.”</li></ul>
<ul><li>Even in his SCJP course (~2 months): he uses short in this class only. Out of two months he never otherwise says the word short.</li></ul>
<h4>Size and range</h4>
<p>Same sign-bit story as byte, scaled up: 1 bit for sign, 15 bits for value.</p>
<p>2¹⁵ = 32,768.</p>
<h4>`short` assignment quizzes (same pattern as `byte`)</h4>
<p>He does not re-explain String here; the pattern is the same as byte.</p>
<h3>[53:55] When is `short` the best choice?</h3>
<p>Rarely used now, but there is a historical reason.</p>
<ul><li>Java came in 1995.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Popular processors then: 8085 / 8086 — 16-bit processors.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Meaning of 16-bit processor: instruction length is 16 bits; data is represented in 16-bit form.</li></ul>
<ul><li>short is 16 bits. Expected form and provided form match → read and write operations become very efficient.</li></ul>
<p>Board:</p>
<p>short data type is best suitable for 16-bit processors like 8085.</p>
<p>But these processors are completely outdated.</p>
<p>Hence the corresponding short data type is also an outdated data type.</p>
<p>Today 32-bit and 64-bit processors are common. Nobody uses 16-bit processors, so short is almost outdated.</p>
<h3>[57:39] `int` data type</h3>
<h4>First point</h4>
<p>`int` is the most commonly used data type in Java.</p>
<p>Even to represent the value 10, the hand always goes to int x = 10; even though byte is enough for 10.</p>
<h4>Size and range</h4>
<p>He groups the digits the Indian way on the board: 2147483648 and 2147483647.</p>
<h3>[1:00:31] `int` quizzes — a *new* compile error</h3>
<p>Max int is 2147483647.</p>
<p>int x = 2147483647;   // valid — the max value
System.out.println(x); // 2147483647</p>
<p>int x = 2147483648;   // invalid</p>
<p>Students predict: possible loss of precision, found long, required int.</p>
<p>Wrong. You do not get that message here.</p>
<h4>Rule (very important)</h4>
<p>Every integral number (number without a decimal point) is by default considered `int` type.</p>
<p>The compiler requires that integral literal to be within the `int` range. If it is not in the int range:</p>
<p>`integer number too large`</p>
<p>2147483648 is still being treated as an int literal. It does not fit. Error: integer number too large.</p>
<h4>How to make it a `long` literal: suffix `l` or `L`</h4>
<p>Only when you suffix with small l or capital L is the literal treated as long.</p>
<p>int x = 2147483648L;   // still invalid for int, but a *different* error</p>
<p>Now the compiler sees a `long` value assigned to int:</p>
<p>`possible loss of precision` — found: long, required: int</p>
<p>He compiles live:</p>
<ol><li>2147483647 → runs, prints the number.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Change last digit to make 2147483648 → integer number too large.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Add l / L → error changes to possible loss of precision found long required int.</li></ol>
<p>You cannot “solve” storing that magnitude in an int — it exceeds the int range. The L suffix only changes how the compiler classifies the literal, not whether int can hold it.</p>
<p>Default: every integral number is int unless suffixed with l/L.</p>
<p>He ends: this is about the int data type — most commonly used; know size and range. `long` continues in the next lecture.</p>
<h2>Code from the lecture</h2>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // --- strongly typed: these do not compile in Java (they would in C) ---
        // int x = 10.5;
        // boolean b = 0;

        // --- byte ---
        byte b1 = 10;     // OK, prints 10
        byte b2 = 127;    // OK, max byte
        // byte b3 = 128;          // CE: possible loss of precision, found int, required byte
        // byte b4 = 10.5;         // CE: possible loss of precision, found double, required byte
        // byte b5 = true;         // CE: incompatible types, found boolean, required byte
        // byte b6 = &quot;durga&quot;;      // CE: incompatible types, found java.lang.String, required byte

        // --- short ---
        short s1 = 32767; // OK, max short
        // short s2 = 32768;       // CE: possible loss of precision, found int, required short
        // short s3 = 10.5;        // CE: possible loss of precision, found double, required short
        // short s4 = true;        // CE: incompatible types, found boolean, required short

        // --- int ---
        int x1 = 2147483647;       // OK, max int
        // int x2 = 2147483648;    // CE: integer number too large
        // int x3 = 2147483648L;   // CE: possible loss of precision, found long, required int
        // int x4 = true;          // CE: incompatible types, found boolean, required int

        System.out.println(b1);
        System.out.println(s1);
        System.out.println(x1);
    }
}</pre>
<h2>Rules to memorize</h2>
<ol><li>Every variable and every expression has a type. Every assignment is checked for type compatibility. → Java is strongly typed.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Interview: Java is not a pure OOP language (missing OOP features + primitives are non-objects). Prefer no in the interview room.</li></ol>
<ol><li>8 primitives: byte, short, int, long, float, double, boolean, char.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Integral = byte short int long. Floating-point = float double. Non-numeric = boolean, char.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Except boolean and char, remaining types are signed.</li></ol>
<ol><li>`byte`: 1 byte / 8 bits; range −128 to +127; MSB is sign bit (0 = +, 1 = −); positives stored directly; negatives in two’s complement.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Out of range number → `possible loss of precision`. Completely different type → `incompatible types`.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Integral literals are `int` by default. 10.5 is `double` by default.</li></ol>
<ol><li>`byte` best choice: stream data (file / network). FileOutputStream.write takes byte[].</li></ol>
<ol><li>`short`: 2 bytes / 16 bits; −32,768 to 32,767; most rarely used; meant for 16-bit processors (8085); now outdated.</li></ol>
<ol><li>`int`: 4 bytes / 32 bits; −2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647; most commonly used.</li></ol>
<ol><li>An unsuffixed integral literal outside int range → `integer number too large` (not “found long”).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Suffix l or L makes a `long` literal; assigning that to int → `possible loss of precision`, found long, required int.</li></ol>
<h2>Exam / interview traps</h2>
<ul><li>int x = 10.5; and boolean b = 0; are illegal in Java, legal in C. Classic “strongly typed” examples.</li></ul>
<ul><li>“Strongly typed” ≠ typing hard on the keyboard.</li></ul>
<ul><li>“Is Java pure OOP?” — both relative-yes and absolute-no are argued in class; recommended interview answer is no, with reasons ready.</li></ul>
<ul><li>byte b = 128; is not incompatible types. It is `possible loss of precision` (found int).</li></ul>
<ul><li>byte b = true; is incompatible types.</li></ul>
<ul><li>byte b = &quot;durga&quot;; found type is `java.lang.String`, not some “string primitive.”</li></ul>
<ul><li>“Use byte because the number is between −128 and 127” is not the best-choice answer he wants. Best choice = streams.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Do not say you use short all the time. He wants: rarely used / outdated 16-bit type.</li></ul>
<ul><li>int x = 2147483648; → `integer number too large`, not possible loss of precision.</li></ul>
<ul><li>int x = 2147483648L; → `possible loss of precision`. The L is the whole difference.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Prefer capital `L` in real code so it is not confused with digit 1 (he allows small l or capital L).</li></ul>
<ul><li>He demonstrated errors on JDK 1.6; wording may differ on newer compilers (possible lossy conversion) but the found/required types stay the same.</li></ul>
<h2>Recap</h2>
<p>This lecture fixes the type system story (strong typing, not-pure-OOP, eight primitives, signed vs boolean/char) and then completes `byte`, `short`, and `int` with size, range, compile-error families, and “best choice” reasons. Next lecture continues with `long`, then floating-point, boolean, char, default values, and the summary table.</p>
<p>Next video: 003-data-types-part-2.md</p>
